Node Breakdown
1 & 2. Dual Triggers + Merge
A monthly cron trigger fires on the 1st of each month at 8:00 AM Guatemala time.
A manual trigger runs in parallel for testing and on-demand execution. Both feed
into a Merge node that normalizes the trigger source — downstream nodes don't need
to know which trigger fired.

3. Google Sheets — Read Transactions
Reads the full transactions sheet with UNFORMATTED_VALUE for numbers (preventing
locale-formatted strings like "1,234.56" from breaking numeric calculations) and
FORMATTED_STRING for dates (preserving human-readable date context). This output
formatting configuration is critical for downstream JavaScript accuracy.

4. Code — KPI Calculator (JavaScript)
The computational core of the workflow. A custom JavaScript function that:

Reads all transaction rows from the Google Sheets node
Classifies each row by account_type and category into three buckets:

Income → accumulated as ventas
COGS → accumulated as cogs (matches multiple category naming conventions)
Expense → accumulated as gastosOp, also tracked by category


Calculates six financial KPIs:

javascriptmargen_bruto_pct  = ((ventas - cogs) / ventas) * 100
utilidad_neta     = ventas - cogs - gastosOp
margen_neto_pct   = (utilidad_neta / ventas) * 100

Generates a top_3_gastos array — the three expense categories with highest
spend, sorted descending
Returns a single normalized JSON object for the AI agent

Key design decisions:

toNum helper with regex sanitization — strips any currency symbols, spaces,
or formatting characters from numeric fields before calculation. Handles messy
real-world spreadsheet data where amounts may be stored as "Q 1,234" or "1234.00"
Multi-label COGS detection — matches account_type === 'cogs',
category === 'cogs', category === 'costo de venta', and category === 'costo'.
Real client data rarely uses perfectly consistent category naming
executeOnce: true — the node processes all rows in a single execution rather
than row-by-row, which is required for aggregation calculations
Rounding before output — all GTQ amounts are rounded to integers, percentages
to 2 decimal places, before passing to the AI. This prevents the AI from receiving
floating point noise like 45231.999999997


5. AI Agent — GPT-5 Financial Analyst
Receives the structured KPI object and generates a financial analysis following a
strict JSON output contract. The agent operates with two prompt layers:
System message (analyst persona + output schema):
Defines the agent as a financial analyst for Guatemalan SMEs (GTQ currency, 12% VAT
context), provides the exact JSON schema it must return, and includes internal
evaluation criteria hidden from the output:

Gross margin: 30–45% healthy, <25% alert, >50% atypical
Net margin: 8–20% healthy, <5% alert
COGS high → gross margin impact; OPEX high → net margin impact

User message (data injection):
Passes the exact calculated values from the Code node using n8n expressions,
with an explicit instruction to use these exact numbers — not zeros, not
approximations. This prevents a common failure mode where the AI ignores provided
data and generates plausible-sounding but fabricated numbers.
Required JSON output:
json{
  "resumen_kpis": { ...exact numbers... },
  "headline": "one-line diagnosis",
  "insights": ["max 2 points"],
  "acciones_sugeridas": ["max 2 concrete actions"]
}
Model choice: GPT-5 — the financial interpretation task benefits from the
strongest available model. Unlike the WhatsApp agent (which uses GPT-4.1-mini for
cost efficiency at high frequency), this workflow runs once per month and produces
a high-stakes business deliverable. Model quality justifies the cost.

6. Code1 — HTML Email Formatter (JavaScript)
Parses the AI agent's JSON output and renders it as a formatted HTML email body
with a plain-text fallback. Handles:

JSON extraction from raw agent output (finds the first { and last } to handle
any preamble the agent might add despite instructions)
Guatemalan locale number formatting (Q1,234 format)
Percentage formatting to 2 decimal places
HTML table for KPIs, bullet lists for insights and actions
Auto-generated subject line with current date: Reporte financiero – 2025-10-01


7. Dual Output — Outlook + Telegram (Parallel)
Both delivery nodes receive the same formatted output simultaneously:

Microsoft Outlook — sends the full HTML report to the client's email
Telegram — sends the plain-text version as a push notification

Why both? Email is the formal record; Telegram is the immediate alert. The client
gets the full formatted report in their inbox and a push notification on their phone
at the same moment — no need to check email to know the report arrived.

Two-Stage AI Pipeline Design
This workflow demonstrates a pattern worth highlighting explicitly:
StageNodeResponsibilityStage 1JavaScript (Code)Precise numerical computation — KPIs, aggregations, top-NStage 2AI Agent (GPT-5)Contextual interpretation — diagnosis, insights, actions
Why not let the AI calculate the KPIs?
AI models are unreliable at arithmetic on real data. Passing raw transaction rows
to an LLM and asking it to calculate gross margin introduces precision errors and
hallucination risk. JavaScript calculates with deterministic accuracy; the AI
receives pre-calculated numbers and focuses exclusively on interpretation.
Why not skip the AI and just send the numbers?
Raw KPI tables require financial literacy to interpret. The AI converts numbers into
actionable language — a headline diagnosis, two insights, two concrete actions — that
a non-financial business owner can act on immediately.

Key Takeaways

Separate computation from interpretation — JavaScript for numbers, AI for
narrative. Never ask an LLM to do arithmetic on real data; never ask a code node
to write business prose.
Strict JSON contracts between nodes — defining the exact schema the AI must
return (in the system prompt) and injecting exact values (in the user message)
produces reliable, parseable outputs. Vague prompts produce inconsistent structures
that break downstream formatting nodes.
UNFORMATTED_VALUE for Google Sheets numeric reads is non-negotiable for
calculation workflows. Locale-formatted numbers break JavaScript arithmetic silently.
Multi-label category matching handles the reality that client data is never
perfectly consistent. Always match multiple naming conventions for critical
classifications like COGS.
Dual delivery (email + Telegram) serves different consumption patterns:
email for the formal record and detailed review, Telegram for immediate awareness.
Both add value; neither is redundant.
Model selection by task frequency and stakes — GPT-5 for a monthly
high-stakes deliverable; GPT-4.1-mini for a high-frequency conversational agent.
Match model cost to execution frequency and output importance.
